  'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>By late July in Temple, the afternoon sun has been beating on your roof for weeks, and highs in the upper 90s to 100s °F are routine. Most homeowners feel that heat in the living room and see it on the electric bill. Fewer think about what it does to the shingles, the decking, and the attic space in between. Good <strong>attic ventilation in Texas</strong> is one of the least glamorous parts of a roofing system, but it has a lot to do with how long your roof lasts and how hard your air conditioner has to work.</p>

<h2>What Summer Heat Does to a Central Texas Roof</h2>

<p>A roof in Bell County deals with three heat-related stresses at the same time: high surface temperatures, intense ultraviolet (UV) light, and big daily temperature swings. Each one wears on roofing materials in a different way.</p>

<h3>Thermal cycling</h3>

<p>On a clear summer day, dark shingles can get far hotter than the air around them. After sunset they cool off, and if a thunderstorm rolls through in the afternoon, the surface temperature can drop sharply in minutes. Every time the roof heats and cools, the materials expand and contract. Over years, that repeated movement loosens nails, stresses the seal strips that hold shingle tabs down, opens small gaps around flashing, and dries out sealants and rubber pipe boots. Metal roofs move too, which is why fastener and panel design matter so much on <a href="/services/metal-roofing-temple-tx/">metal roofing</a>.</p>

<h3>UV exposure and granule loss</h3>

<p>The colored granules on an asphalt shingle are not just decoration. They shield the asphalt underneath from sunlight. As UV and heat break the asphalt down, it becomes brittle and holds the granules less firmly. Rain and wind wash them loose, and you start seeing them in gutters and at the bottom of downspouts. Once the asphalt is exposed, it ages faster still. Some shedding is normal on a new roof; heavy, steady loss on an older one is a sign of age.</p>

<h3>Heat from below</h3>

<p>Here is the part many people miss: a shingle is being heated from both sides. The sun cooks it from above, and a poorly ventilated attic can hold heat against the underside of the roof deck all afternoon and well into the evening. That trapped heat accelerates the same aging process and can contribute to blistering, curling, and premature cracking.</p>

<h2>How Attic Ventilation in Texas Homes Is Supposed to Work</h2>

<p>A typical vented attic works on a simple idea: cooler outside air comes in low, warmer attic air goes out high. Hot air naturally rises, and wind blowing across the roof helps pull air out at the top. When intake and exhaust are both present and sized correctly, air moves through the whole attic instead of stagnating.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Intake vents</strong> sit at the lowest part of the roof, usually in the soffits (the underside of the eaves). Homes with little or no overhang can use special intake vents near the roof edge.</li>
  <li><strong>Exhaust vents</strong> sit at or near the highest part of the roof. Ridge vents, static box vents (sometimes called turtle vents), turbine vents, gable vents, and powered attic fans are all exhaust options.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why balance between intake and exhaust matters</h3>

<p>One of the most common ventilation problems roofers run into is not a shortage of exhaust. It is a shortage of intake. A house can have a long ridge vent across the top, but if the soffit vents were painted over, blocked by blown-in insulation, or never installed, that ridge vent has very little fresh air to pull through. The attic stays hot no matter how much exhaust is on the roof.</p>

<p>As a general rule of thumb, builders aim for an intake capacity that is equal to or a bit greater than the exhaust capacity. Too much exhaust and too little intake can also cause exhaust vents to pull air from places you do not want, such as from inside the house through ceiling gaps and light fixtures, which pulls conditioned air into the attic.</p>

<h2>Common Sizing Rules of Thumb: 1/150 and 1/300</h2>

<p>Ventilation is measured in <em>net free area</em> (NFA), the actual open area air can pass through after accounting for screens, louvers, and baffles. Each vent product lists its NFA, usually in square inches.</p>

<p>Two ratios appear in widely used building codes and industry guidance:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>1/150:</strong> about 1 square foot of net free area for every 150 square feet of attic floor space.</li>
  <li><strong>1/300:</strong> about 1 square foot of NFA for every 300 square feet of attic floor, generally allowed only when certain conditions are met, such as having a balanced split between intake and exhaust with the exhaust located high on the roof.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is a rough example of how the math works for a home with an 1,800-square-foot attic floor:</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Ratio</th><th>Total NFA needed</th><th>Approx. intake (half)</th><th>Approx. exhaust (half)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>1/150</td><td>12 sq ft (1,728 sq in)</td><td>864 sq in</td><td>864 sq in</td></tr>
    <tr><td>1/300</td><td>6 sq ft (864 sq in)</td><td>432 sq in</td><td>432 sq in</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table></div>

<div class="callout">These ratios are general guidance, not a design for your house. The adopted local building code and the shingle manufacturer's installation requirements are what actually govern a project. Attic shape, roof pitch, insulation, and whether the attic is vented or sealed all change the answer. A roofer should check the specifics before recommending vent changes.</div>

<p>If you are curious about your roof's slope, which affects how certain vents perform, our <a href="/tools/roof-pitch-calculator/">roof pitch calculator</a> can give you a quick estimate.</p>

<!--cta-->

<h2>Why You Should Not Mix Exhaust Types</h2>

<p>It seems logical that more vents equal a cooler attic. In practice, combining different exhaust types on the same attic space often makes things worse. Air takes the path of least resistance. If a house has a ridge vent and a powered attic fan, the fan can pull air <em>in</em> through the nearest section of ridge vent instead of through the soffits. The result is a short circuit: air cycles in and out near the top of the roof while the lower attic stays hot. Rain can even get drawn in through an exhaust vent that is suddenly acting as an intake.</p>

<p>The same thing can happen with gable vents paired with a ridge vent, or with box vents placed at different heights. General industry guidance is to choose one type of exhaust for each continuous attic space and size it properly. When a roof is replaced, sorting out a patchwork of old turbines, box vents, and fans should be part of the plan. A <a href="/services/roof-replacement-temple-tx/">roof replacement</a> is the easiest time to correct ventilation, since the old materials are already coming off.</p>

<h2>Radiant Barriers: Helpful, but Not a Replacement for Ventilation</h2>

<p>A radiant barrier is a reflective foil layer, either stapled to the underside of the rafters or built into the roof decking itself. It reflects a large share of the radiant heat coming off the hot roof deck so less of it reaches the attic floor and insulation. In hot, sunny climates like Central Texas, radiant barriers are popular and can help reduce attic heat gain.</p>

<p>A few points to keep in mind:</p>

<ul>
  <li>A radiant barrier works best with an air gap on its reflective side and does not replace the need for intake and exhaust airflow.</li>
  <li>Radiant barrier decking can be installed during a full replacement, when the old decking is replaced anyway.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Signs Your Attic Is Not Breathing Well</h2>

<p>You do not need to climb onto the roof to spot ventilation trouble. Most clues show up from the ground, inside the house, or from a safe look into the attic.</p>

<div class="callout callout--warn">Never walk on your roof to check vents. Summer roof surfaces can be hot enough to burn skin, and steep or granule-covered shingles are slippery. In the attic, step only on framing or a floored walkway, watch for nails, and keep visits short on hot afternoons. Attic temperatures in summer can be dangerous.</div>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Upstairs rooms that never cool off</strong>, even when the thermostat is set low.</li>
  <li><strong>An AC that runs nearly nonstop</strong> on summer afternoons, along with rising electric bills compared with previous summers.</li>
  <li><strong>Shingles that look curled, blistered, or prematurely worn</strong>, especially on the sunniest slopes, when the roof is not that old.</li>
  <li><strong>Heavy granule buildup</strong> in gutters or at the base of downspouts.</li>
  <li><strong>Soffit vents that are painted shut</strong>, covered with solid panels, or blocked from the inside by insulation.</li>
  <li><strong>Rusty nail tips, dark staining, or mildew</strong> on the underside of the roof deck, which can point to moisture that is not escaping. Winter humidity matters too, not just summer heat.</li>
  <li><strong>Wavy or sagging decking</strong> visible from the ground, which can come from long-term heat and moisture exposure.</li>
</ul>

<p>If you notice stains on your ceilings, our article on <a href="/blog/common-causes-of-roof-leaks-central-texas/">common causes of roof leaks in Central Texas</a> covers what else could be going on.</p>

<h2>How Ventilation Affects Shingle Life and Energy Use</h2>

<h3>Shingle life</h3>

<p>Heat speeds up the chemical aging of asphalt, which is why attic ventilation in Texas matters more than in milder climates. A roof that sits over a well-ventilated attic generally runs cooler on the underside than one over a stagnant, superheated attic. Over many Texas summers, that difference adds up. For a broader look at what shortens or extends roof life locally, see <a href="/blog/how-long-does-a-roof-last-temple-texas/">how long a roof lasts in Temple, Texas</a>.</p>

<p>Many shingle manufacturers also spell out ventilation requirements in their installation instructions, and inadequate ventilation can affect warranty coverage, so it is worth reading your product paperwork.</p>

<h3>Energy use</h3>

<p>Your attic sits directly on top of your ceiling insulation and, in many Temple homes, on top of the air conditioning ductwork and air handler as well. When the attic is extremely hot, heat pushes down through the insulation and warms the air moving through the ducts before it ever reaches your rooms. Better ventilation, adequate insulation, sealed ceiling penetrations, and properly sealed ducts all work together. A hot, stagnant attic makes every other weakness worse.</p>

<h2>Practical Steps for Temple Homeowners</h2>

<p>Older homes in central Temple near downtown were often built with fewer vents than modern code calls for, and later additions may have created separate attic spaces with their own airflow needs. Newer subdivisions in west and south Temple usually started with a ventilation plan, but blown-in insulation, added fans, or a quick re-roof can throw it off balance. Either way, a few simple steps go a long way:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Walk around the house and look up at the soffits. Make sure you can see vent openings and that they are not painted over or clogged with debris or wasp nests.</li>
  <li>If your attic is safely accessible, check early in the morning that insulation is not stuffed against the soffit openings. Baffles (rafter vents) keep that channel open.</li>
  <li>Count the types of exhaust vents on your roof from the ground. A mix of turbines, box vents, a ridge vent, and a fan is a red flag for short-circuiting.</li>
  <li>Keep gutters clear so granule loss and drainage problems are easy to spot. Our <a href="/services/gutters-roof-drainage-temple-tx/">gutters and roof drainage</a> team can help if they are not draining well.</li>
  <li>Plan attic ventilation upgrades alongside roofing work. Adding or changing vents during a replacement or larger <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a> is usually simpler and cleaner than doing it as a separate project.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Getting a Clear Answer for Your Attic</h2>

<p>If your upstairs is hard to cool, your shingles look older than they should, or you are planning a new roof and want the ventilation done right, a professional look is the fastest way to know where you stand. Temple Roofers can check your intake and exhaust from the ground and the attic, photograph what we find, and explain your options with a written estimate. You can <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">schedule a free roof inspection</a> or learn more about our <a href="/services/roof-inspection-temple-tx/">roof inspection service</a> before summer heat takes another year off your roof.</p>
